    <h1>Discord Bot Privacy Policy: What Discord Requires and How to Write One</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published August 27, 2026 &middot; Updated September 10, 2026</p>

    <p>Discord requires every bot registered in the Developer Portal to have a privacy policy under its Developer Policy, and a working policy URL becomes an enforced gate once your bot needs verification -- triggered at <strong>75 servers</strong> -- or applies for the App Directory. The policy has to describe what your bot actually does: which user IDs, guild IDs, and message content it processes, which privileged intents it uses and why, and what happens to any of it after the bot leaves a server. A generic document copied from a website's terms page, one that never names the bot, is one of the fastest ways to get a verification application rejected.</p>

    <p>This guide covers exactly what Discord's Trust & Safety team checks during verification, the specific rejection scenarios developers run into and how to fix each one, what data your bot handles that goes undisclosed most often, how the 2026 split between bot verification and privileged intent review actually works, and what changes -- technically and legally -- once your bot crosses the 75-server line. Rather generate a policy than write one from scratch? <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">Generate a privacy policy for free</a> and host it at no cost.</p>

