    <h2 id="private-team-bots">Privacy Policies for Private and Team-Only Bots</h2>

    <p>Not every Discord bot is headed for the App Directory or a large public server base. Three categories cover most bots that never see verification at all: internal team tools never invited to an external server, bots that stay intentionally small and never approach 75 servers, and bots still in active testing or development.</p>

    <h3>Internal team tools</h3>

    <p>A bot built to run in a single company's Discord server -- handling deploy notifications, standup reminders, or internal ticketing -- never gets invited anywhere else and never approaches the verification threshold. Discord does not require or check a privacy policy for a bot like this at any point in its lifecycle, since it never triggers verification or Directory review.</p>

    <p>That said, organizational compliance may require one regardless of what Discord enforces. If the bot processes employee data -- names, Discord IDs, message content used for internal analytics -- your company's own data-handling policies, especially under GDPR if any team members are in the EU, may obligate you to document the bot's practices internally even without a public-facing requirement from Discord.</p>

    <h3>Small bots that stay under 75 servers by design</h3>

    <p>Some bots are built for a niche community and never intend to scale past a handful of servers. Discord does not enforce a privacy policy requirement for bots that never cross 75 servers, since verification simply never triggers. The Developer Policy still technically requires a policy at any scale, but in practice enforcement is limited to the moments described earlier in this guide: verification, App Directory listing, and (separately) privileged intent review at 10,000 unique users.</p>

    <h3>Testing and development bots</h3>

    <p>A bot running only in your own test server while you build it out has no meaningful audience and no enforcement trigger. Once you start inviting real servers, though, is the right moment to write the policy -- not after you are already capped at 75 servers and rushing through verification. Drafting it while the data flows are still fresh in your head is faster and more accurate than reconstructing them later.</p>

    <p>Whichever category your bot falls into, having a policy in place before you need one costs little and avoids the scramble that comes with racing a verification deadline once your bot's growth outpaces your paperwork.</p>

    <h2 id="host-and-add">Hosting Your Policy and Adding It to the Developer Portal</h2>

    <p>The policy needs a stable, public URL. Google Docs, Notion pages, and Pastebin links are a poor fit -- they can change, go private, or disappear, exactly what Discord's reviewers watch for. Three options hold up:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Your bot's own site</strong> -- a path like <code>yourbotname.com/privacy</code>. Full control, no third-party dependency.</li>
      <li><strong>A free generated policy</strong> -- if you have no website, <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate and host one for free</a> and link straight to the permanent URL.</li>
      <li><strong>GitHub Pages</strong> -- a static HTML page in a public repo, common for open-source bots, as long as it renders without a GitHub login.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where the fields live in the Developer Portal</h3>

    <p>Open <a href="https://discord.com/developers/applications" rel="nofollow">discord.com/developers/applications</a> and select your application. The <strong>General Information</strong> tab holds the fields that matter here: <strong>Name</strong>, <strong>Description</strong>, <strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong>, <strong>Terms of Service URL</strong>, and <strong>Tags</strong> (up to five). Paste the full URL, including <code>https://</code>, into <strong>Privacy Policy URL</strong>, and do the same for <strong>Terms of Service URL</strong> if you have one -- both are checked together during verification and both later appear on your bot's public profile once verified.</p>

    <p>Separately, the <strong>Bot</strong> tab holds the <strong>Privileged Gateway Intents</strong> section, where the Presence, Server Members, and Message Content toggles live -- this is where you actually enable the intents your policy needs to describe. The <strong>Installation</strong> tab configures OAuth2 scopes and bot permissions for the invite link, which is worth double-checking against your policy too: a bot requesting the <code>administrator</code> permission or a broad OAuth2 scope set should have a policy that justifies why.</p>

    <p>These URLs also surface during your bot's OAuth2 authorization flow, and they are the same fields verification and Directory reviewers check. If your data practices change, update the policy and bump the modified date before your next reapplication or Directory review, not after.</p>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Does every Discord bot need a privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Discord's Developer Policy requires one for every application, bots included, from registration onward -- enforcement is just heaviest at verification (75+ servers) and for App Directory listing.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need one if my bot doesn't store any data?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Your bot still receives user IDs and usernames through the API on every event, even with no database, which is still processing under GDPR -- say data is processed but not persisted, if accurate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>At how many servers does my Discord bot need to be verified?</h3>
        <p>At 75 servers. Discord lowered this from 100 servers in its 2024 policy update. An unverified bot cannot accept invites to more servers past that point, and verification requires a working privacy policy URL, a terms of service URL, an accurate bot description, justified privileged intent usage, and completed identity verification for the team owner.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What changed with the Message Content Intent review in 2026?</h3>
        <p>Discord separated bot verification from privileged intent review. Bot verification is triggered at 75 servers and is a one-time process. Privileged intent review now runs on unique user count instead: under 10,000 users, you can still self-toggle Message Content, Server Members, and Presence intents; past that, you have 90 days to apply and must reapply annually.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Why does the Developer Portal still say 100 servers next to the privileged intent toggles?</h3>
        <p>The note under Presence, Server Members, and Message Content in the Bot tab is leftover copy from before Discord's 2024 change and still references 100 servers. The bot verification trigger that actually applies today is 75 servers; privileged intent review is a separate process tied to unique user count, not server count at all.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What does Discord's verification review actually check?</h3>
        <p>A valid Privacy Policy URL and Terms of Service URL in General Information, an accurate bot description, justified usage of any enabled privileged intent, and completed Stripe identity verification for the team owner. Discord's Trust & Safety team reviews the application manually.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Why was my bot's verification application rejected?</h3>
        <p>The most common reasons are a missing or broken privacy policy URL, a policy that doesn't describe the bot's actual data practices, privileged intents enabled without a clear justification, incomplete identity verification for the team owner, or a bot description that doesn't match what the bot actually does.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What happens if my bot stays under 75 servers?</h3>
        <p>Discord does not enforce the verification checklist, since it never triggers. The privacy policy requirement in the Developer Policy technically still applies at any scale, but enforcement in practice is limited to verification, App Directory listing, and privileged intent review at 10,000 unique users.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Does my bot need to be verified to appear in the App Directory?</h3>
        <p>Yes. App Directory listing requires the bot to already be verified, to have a Privacy Policy URL and Terms of Service URL set in General Information, and to pass a separate App Directory review on top of verification itself.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Where do I add my bot's privacy policy URL?</h3>
        <p>Developer Portal &rsaquo; your application &rsaquo; General Information &rsaquo; Privacy Policy URL -- the same field verification and App Directory review check.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can I host my bot's privacy policy on a GitHub Gist or Google Doc?</h3>
        <p>It is discouraged -- both can change ownership or be edited without notice. Your own domain, a hosted generator page, or GitHub Pages are more stable.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Do I need a separate privacy policy for my bot and my website?</h3>
        <p>Not necessarily. One policy can cover both if it specifically describes each surface's data practices, with the bot-relevant sections actually present and accurate.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Does a private, internal-only bot need a privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Discord does not check for one since a bot that never leaves a single organization's server never triggers verification or Directory review. Your organization's own compliance requirements may still call for one, especially if the bot processes employee data under GDPR.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
